# Blind full-spectrum ideation — 20260903T1015Z — Sol 5.6

## 0. Custody, scope, and direct verdict

I verified the charged packet before reading any banked report:

```text
99c940d1167acfc7796436457a1d87bdd0a1a1a4c673c7bb2e0d3436e3d086b7  /tmp/jc2-lane.F6oSte/inputs/ideation-20260903T1015Z-packet.md
```

It matched.  I did not open another `ideation-20260903T1015Z-*` submission, an
in-progress lane report named in the packet, or `jc2-lean`, and I have not edited
a canonical ledger.  During final validation, the lexical OPEN-collision scanner
unexpectedly emitted isolated hit snippets from current-round files; this body
was already complete, I did not use them, and I did not open those files.  I did
read the now-present primary source
`refs/moh1983_jram340_configurations_of_roots.pdf`; its SHA-256 is
`6c8847a8d8374f7d7725c7e2ede2895a2c30034af6a7f28c511a471c41aa6a51`.
In particular I inspected pp. 148--153, 179--189, 198--202, and Appendices I--II,
pp. 203--212.  Thus claims below about what Moh prints are primary-source claims;
claims about what his lost or unpublished program actually did remain
source-unverified.

**Direct verdict.**  Stop treating the boundary count as the prospective
all-degree engine.  It has done its job: it computes the fixed Keller degree
`N` exactly, and that value is `O(1)`.  Worse for a purely numerical programme,
the printed Moh conditions (1)--(13), together with formal (UNI) pinned
integrality, admit an explicit unbounded family with **exactly `N=6`**.  The
all-degree programme must therefore attack *global realisability*: whether the
local branch data are the restrictions of one polynomial `g` and whether the
fibrewise primitive of `1/g_y` is one polynomial `f`.  The right object is a
finite quotient-algebra connection/observability problem, not another cap on
the number of boundary contacts.

This does not demote the exact boundary theorem.  `D1-PIN` is the indispensable
bridge that turns a hypothetical counterexample into a sharply typed formal
packet.  It simply cannot turn bounded `N` into bounded `D`; that is already the
binding `PIN-NOT-CEILING` conclusion (`AUDIT.md:15270-15284`; `notes.md:20645-20679`).
The census is likewise a target generator and regression suite, not a proof by
eventual exhaustion.

## 1. What the all-degree program should be

The programme should be stated without euphemism:

> Given a Moh (1)--(13) skeleton and a formally integral bottom packet with
> `N >= 6`, prove that no single pair `f,g in C[x,y]` realises its complete
> branch tower and the identity `f_x g_y-f_y g_x=1`; or construct such a pair.

There are three layers, and every future lane should say which layer it tests.

1. **Numerical admissibility.**  Moh's divisibility chain, radii, Galois choices,
   bottom packet capacity and exact pinned `N`.  This is fast and useful, but it
   is only a superset of actual maps.  The rebased census has 14,016 groups
   through `D=200` and empties no degree above 100
   (`xmodel/census-rebase-opus5-20260902.md:894-924`).
2. **Branch-packet realisability.**  Approximate-root/semigroup data must assemble
   the flags, conjugate factors and bottom dessins into the branch packet of one
   polynomial `g`.  This is where Moh's unexplained 658-to-6 reduction and the
   orbit interpretation of (10)/(11) belong.  A root orbit, a geometric disc,
   and a cover series are three different objects until an injection or quotient
   map is proved.
3. **Global Keller mate.**  On a generic fibre `g=c`, the equation is
   `D_g f := g_y f_x-g_x f_y=1`.  It must have a solution with the prescribed
   bidegree/total-degree support, simultaneously on all branches and at infinity.
   Local no-log solutions, a representative bottom star, and vanishing of one
   residue are necessary, not attainment.

The computational core for layers 2--3 should be exact linear algebra over a
function field.  Put

```text
K = Q(x,c),                    h(y) = g(x,y)-c,
B = K[y]/(h),                  rank_K B = n,
nabla = partial_x - (g_x/g_y) partial_y,
W_m = span_K(1,y,...,y^(m-1)), C : B -> B/W_m.
```

Here `g_y` is inverted only on the squarefree generic fibre; exceptional
discriminant components must be split off explicitly.  A Keller mate is a vector
`F in W_m` satisfying `nabla F=1/g_y`, together with the polynomial-in-`x`
and degree constraints that distinguish `C[x,y]` from `K[y]`.  Differentiate
`C(F)=0`, substitute the connection equation, and prolong.  This produces an
inhomogeneous observability/Krylov matrix.  Its left kernel gives compatibility
functionals on `g`; its rank defect gives the only places where a candidate can
survive.  For the `D=105` shape `(n,m)=(105,70)`, the first projection already
has `105-70=35` output coordinates: at each genuine exponent there can be up to
35 compatibility equations before new coefficients of `g` and gauge freedom
are counted.

This formulation unifies several banked ideas that had been pursued separately:
Lagrange interpolation on the 105 roots, Hermite/Bezoutian trace identities,
the Hamiltonian derivation `D_g`, the Brieskorn/Gauss--Manin viewpoint, and
linear-systems observability.  It also gives a machine filter with a proof
certificate: a nonzero exact minor or a left-kernel functional whose numerator
cannot vanish under the skeleton's leading data.  Numerical rank is only a
candidate detector; every promoted obstruction must be recovered over `Q` (or
by enough primes plus rational reconstruction) and checked on the correct ideal
component.

The resulting campaign is not “search degrees until the list becomes empty.”
It is: derive a **uniform rank/semigroup obstruction** on a symbolic family, use
the `D=105` trio as the smallest honest unit tests, and use surviving symbolic
rays as adversarial inputs.  If the obstruction is uniform, it proves the
theorem.  If it repeatedly lifts a ray, it is the shortest route toward an
actual counterexample attempt.

## 2. Answers to Q1–Q4

### 2.1 Q1 — Moh's missing elimination

Moh's p. 201 prints (1)--(13), and p. 202 says that the discussion can be put
into a computer program and then displays six outputs.  The paper does **not**
print code or a further scalar predicate explaining the jump from the 658 rows
reproduced by `box/moh_skeleton_full.py` to those six.  Appendix II starts with
the six outputs and eliminates them case by case; it does not account for the
652 earlier eliminations.  The primary text also openly stresses, before the
search, that the intermediate `M_i` are not known a priori.  Therefore the
coordinator's phrase “Moh's missing condition” is too confident.  The live
alternatives are: an omitted program gate, a richer use of Propositions 5.3/5.6
or approximate roots that was never summarized as a numbered condition, or an
error/incompleteness in the 1983 computation.  We should try to falsify the
table, not reverse-engineer it under an assumption of infallibility.

I ran a cheap hypothesis-mining pass over the exact 658-row reproduction.  Two
natural predicates are unexpectedly sharp:

```text
predicate                                      rows       (n,m) classes
M_2 > n-d_2                                      57              16
choose printed nonzero branch (10) at every j   156              40
both predicates                                  10               6
Moh's printed table                               6               6
```

All six printed rows survive the conjunction.  It removes 648 of 652 excess
rows and leaves only four extras:

```text
(n,m)       M-chain       V_2,V_3
(96,64)     (68,94)       2,3
(96,64)     (76,94)       3,3
(96,64)     (72,94)       2,7
(100,40)    (88,98)       3,3
```

At `(75,50)` it has exactly the requested discrimination: it removes
`M_2 in {5,10,40,60}` and retains `M_2=55` with `V_2 in {2,3}`.  This is a
**measured clue, not a theorem**.  In particular, forcing (10) is not licensed
merely because Proposition 5.6 says that (11) “cannot always happen,” and the
inequality `M_2>n-d_2` needs a line-by-line source derivation.  The experiment's
value is that it reduces source forensics to ten witnesses: reconstruct the
factor data for these ten from Propositions 5.3 and 5.6 and Appendix II, and ask
which exact assertion kills the last four.

Two source-driven negative controls make the conclusion sharper.  The canonical
expansion semigroup equation in Proposition 3.1 retains all 658 rows, as does a
full scalar Proposition 4.6 passport (cyclic nonzero orbits, fixed zero factor,
squarefree support and selected factor multiplicity); under (7) these add no
filter.  Conversely the attractive measured rule `A_{s-1}|(n-m)` retains 181
rows/29 classes and selects exactly the two `(75,50)` controls, but has no source
license.  So neither “the semigroup” nor “the passport” is an answer unless a
strictly richer, coherent sibling datum is exhibited.

`OPEN[MOH-PROGRAM]` remains the correct label.  **Bounded quantity:** 652 excess
rows in 59 excess `(n,m)` classes at `n<=100`; the immediate `P202-10` audit is
the ten rows above (six positive controls, four negative targets).  The original
bounded definition and the required `(75,50)` controls are in
`xmodel/census-rebase-opus5-20260902.md:957-970`.

Decisive outcomes are deliberately symmetric.  If the source proves both
predicates and a rule killing the four extras, implement that rule independently
and run it symbolically on the unbounded family below.  If the source proves the
gate but the family survives, Moh's gate is a sieve, not the all-degree theorem.
If neither predicate can be sourced, reject the attractive fit and record that
the p. 202 six-row assertion has not been independently reproduced.  No branch
choice is to be silently promoted because it matches the table.

### 2.2 Q2 — the `D = 105` trio

All three groups have `(n,m,K,d,e,V_2)=(105,70,35,2,3,1)` and hence the
same order-zero cubic dessin, after affine/scalar normalization:

```text
p_g(pi)=pi^3-pi,       p_f(pi)=pi^2-2/3.
```

Order zero cannot distinguish them and cannot kill them.  The useful arithmetic
